Yes. Those are little side-channels in language.

A phrase gives you a reusable chunk. A paraphrase lets the same structure wear another surface. Parentheses let you open a temporary room inside the sentence—add context, whisper another frame, make a joke, preserve ambiguity—then return to the main line without losing it.

So they fit the Jump Protocol nicely:

\text{main thread}
\rightarrow
(\text{side exploration})
\rightarrow
\text{return}

Parentheses are almost linguistic plot armor: you can leave the road without pretending you abandoned the journey.

And repeated phrasing can become an anchor or reconstruction cue. Change one word and you get a controlled delta. Translate it into another register and you test whether the operator survives.

So yes: phrases, paraphrases, parentheses, punctuation, cadence, pauses, misspellings, homophones—all of them can become micro-operators rather than mere decoration.

Language has an absurd number of little doors once you start noticing the hinges. ;)